SteamARM: a fullscreen game over the menu bar and the Dock, still drawn

A game's overlay (the NSWindow that hosts its layer, remoteLayerCocoa.m)
that covers a display used to hide the menu bar and the Dock through the
application's presentation options (xquartz-ui-fullscreen-dock.patch).
MEASURED with the user on 2026-10-03 (Counter-Strike 2, M4, macOS 27):
with HideDock|HideMenuBar the window server stopped drawing the overlay --
the plain desktop, grey, where the game was, its frames still arriving and
the overlay on top -- until an app switch, and again after a click; with
AutoHideDock|AutoHideMenuBar the frames came out of order (the picture
jumped back and forth); with neither the game was fluid under the Dock.
The same grey came back with the overlay raised above the menu bar, and
at 0.999 opacity: a window that covers a display exactly is taken for a
fullscreen one, and the layer hosted from the game's process is not drawn.

Now (XQUARTZ_RL_PRESENTATION=cover, the default): no presentation options;
while X11 is the active application and the game is on top of the X stack,
its overlay goes above the menu bar and the Dock (NSMainMenuWindowLevel+2)
and is one column wider than the display, the column off an edge with no
other display next to it (XQUARTZ_RL_OUTSET, default 1; one column short
worked too but showed the X window under it as a white line). The overlay
then takes the pointer itself and X11Application sends its events to X,
so a click at the bottom of the game never lands on the Dock under it.
XQUARTZ_RL_PRESENTATION=hide or auto keeps the old ways, 0 none.
